    summary: {
      en: "In medieval Bohemia, beer was much more than a drink. It was the [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]townspeople[[/link]]'s livelihood.\n\nSince the fourteenth century the [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]royal towns[[/link]] had held a privilege: within a mile of their walls, nobody but the townspeople could brew or sell beer. The [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]burghers[[/link]] of Prague, Plzeň and Kutná Hora grew rich on it, and the taverns along their streets were the towns' purses. The [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]lords[[/link]] grew barley and raised livestock, but the brewing trade had never been theirs.\n\nOnce the [[link:the-lords-hold-the-pen-1500]]Vladislav Land Ordinance[[/link]] was in place, the lords decided that this old rule was due for a change too.\n\nThey built breweries on their own estates, brewed their own beer and sold it themselves. Nor was that enough: they forbade the [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]peasants[[/link]] on their estates to buy beer in town and made them drink only what their lord brewed, and they would not even sell their grain to the townspeople. The town breweries ran out of barley, and the town taverns ran out of customers. Watching all this, a chronicler sighed with grief:\n\n[[quote:0]]\n\nThe towns, of course, refused to give in. In November 1502 the townsmen simply announced that until their old rights were restored, they would not attend the [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]diet[[/link]]. The lords' answer was just as simple: they would rather die than give way.\n\n[[b]]One side would rather skip the diet, the other would rather die, and all over a barrel of beer.[[/b]]\n\nIn the end, the quarrel spilled into the streets. The chronicle records one incident: a lord slapped a townsman in the face, and the townsman, not one to hold back, slapped him right back. The lord then sued him before the land court, where every judge was a lord. The case did not cost the townsman his life, but it cost him everything he owned.\n\nTo the townspeople, peace between the lords and the towns sounded like an old joke:\n\n[[quote:1]]\n\nAnd the king? The king was in Buda. The townspeople sent letters to Buda, the lords sent letters to Buda, and the king said \"fine\" to both. The chronicler did not mince words about him:\n\n[[quote:2]]\n\n[[b]]The king said \"fine\" to everyone, so nothing was fine for anyone.[[/b]]\n\nAnd so the townspeople and the lords went on quarrelling, year after year. For a long time it was letters, lawsuits and the odd slap. Blood would not be spilled until a fair in the Old Town, four years later.",
      cz: "Ve středověkých Čechách nebylo pivo jen nápojem. Bylo to živobytí [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]měšťanů[[/link]].\n\n[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]Královská města[[/link]] měla už od 14. století výsadu: v okruhu jedné míle za hradbami nesměl kromě měšťanů nikdo vařit ani prodávat pivo. Měšťané v Praze, Plzni a Kutné Hoře na tom zbohatli a krčmy v ulicích byly městskou pokladnicí. [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]Páni[[/link]] pěstovali ječmen a chovali dobytek, ale k vaření piva se nikdy nedostali.\n\nJakmile platilo [[link:the-lords-hold-the-pen-1500]]Vladislavské zřízení zemské[[/link]], usoudili páni, že by se mělo změnit i tohle staré pravidlo.\n\nNa svých panstvích postavili pivovary, sami vařili a sami prodávali. A to jim nestačilo: [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]poddaným[[/link]] zakázali kupovat pivo ve městě a nechali je pít jen to panské; ani obilí už měšťanům neprodávali. Městské pivovary zůstaly bez ječmene a městské krčmy bez hostů. Kronikář se na to díval a jen bolestně vzdychal:\n\n[[quote:0]]\n\nMěsta se ovšem nehodlala vzdát. V listopadu 1502 měšťané rovnou prohlásili, že dokud jim stará práva nevrátí, na [[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]zemský sněm[[/link]] chodit nebudou. Odpověď pánů byla stejně jednoduchá: raději zemřou, než by ustoupili.\n\n[[b]]Jedni raději nepřijdou na sněm, druzí raději zemřou, a to všechno kvůli sudu piva.[[/b]]\n\nNakonec se začalo bojovat i na ulici. Kronika zaznamenala takovou příhodu: jeden pán dal měšťanovi facku a měšťan se nedal a vrátil mu ji. Pán ho pak pohnal před zemský soud, kde seděli samí páni. Měšťana ten spor nestál život, ale stál ho celý majetek.\n\nMěšťanům připadal smír mezi pány a městy jako starý vtip:\n\n[[quote:1]]\n\nA král? Král byl v Budíně. Měšťané psali do Budína, páni psali do Budína a král oběma říkal „dobře“. Kronikář si o něm nebral servítky:\n\n[[quote:2]]\n\n[[b]]Král říkal „dobře“ všem, a tak nebylo dobře nikomu.[[/b]]\n\nA tak se měšťané s pány hádali dál, rok za rokem. Dlouho to byly jen dopisy, žaloby a sem tam nějaká facka. Krev poteče až o čtyři roky později, na jednom staroměstském trhu.",
      zh: "在中世纪的波希米亚，啤酒可不只是一种饮料。它是[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]城里人[[/link]]的饭碗。\n\n[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]王室城市[[/link]]从十四世纪起就握着一项特权：城墙外一英里之内，除了城里人，谁也不许酿酒卖酒。布拉格、比尔森、库特纳霍拉的[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]市民[[/link]]靠它发家，街上的酒馆就是城市的钱袋子。[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]贵族[[/link]]们种大麦、养牲口，可酿酒这门买卖，一向轮不到他们。\n\n[[link:the-lords-hold-the-pen-1500]]《弗拉迪斯拉夫邦法》[[/link]]一出来，贵族们觉得，这条老规矩也该改改了。\n\n他们在自己的庄园里建起酒坊，自己酿、自己卖。这还不够：他们不许庄园上的[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]农民[[/link]]去城里买啤酒，只许喝领主酿的；连粮食也不许卖给城里人。城里的酒坊没了大麦，城里的酒馆没了客人。编年史家看着这一切，心疼得直叹气：\n\n[[quote:0]]\n\n城市当然不肯认输。1502年11月，市民们干脆宣布：只要旧日的权利一天不还回来，他们就一天不去参加[[link:who-prays-who-fights-who-works-1500]]邦议会[[/link]]。贵族们的回答也很干脆：他们宁可去死，也绝不让步。\n\n[[b]]一边宁可不开会，一边宁可去死，吵的却是一桶啤酒。[[/b]]\n\n吵到后来，连街上都动起了手。编年史里记着这样一件事：一位贵族在城里打了一个市民一记耳光，市民也不客气，回手还了他一巴掌。结果贵族把他告上了邦法院，那里坐的全是贵族。这场官司没要市民的命，却把他的家底打光了。\n\n在城里人眼里，贵族和城市讲和，就像一个老笑话：\n\n[[quote:1]]\n\n那么，国王呢？国王在布达。城里人往布达寄信，贵族们也往布达寄信，国王对两边都说“好”。编年史家对他的评价毫不客气：\n\n[[quote:2]]\n\n[[b]]好好国王对谁都说“好”，所以这场仗，谁也说不“好”。[[/b]]\n\n于是，城里人和贵族就这样一年接一年地吵了下去。吵来吵去，还只是写信、告状、扇耳光。真正见血，要等到四年后老城的一场集市。",
    },
